    {
      "obligation": "refuter:2:2",
      "id": "ex-primes-represented-by-x-squared-plus-two-y-squared",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "Step 2.1 uses [L1] after [L2] produces only 'some form of discriminant -8 primitively represents p'. But [L1] in ex-reduced-forms-of-discriminant-minus-eight concludes only that every primitive positive-definite form of discriminant -8 is properly equivalent to x^2+2y^2. The proof has not established that the form from [L2] is primitive as a form. Those are different notions on disk: primitive representation is about a coprime pair, while primitive form is about common divisors of the coefficients; for example, (2,2,2) primitively represents 2 via (1,0) but is not a primitive form. So the cited result is applied with a dropped hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
